### What is Collectors.mapping?

`Collectors.mapping` is an adapting collector. It takes a mapping function and a downstream collector. It applies the mapper to each incoming element and then hands the mapped value to the downstream collector. It is the collector-level equivalent of a `Stream.map` followed by a `collect`, except it runs inside another collector, once per group.

The signature is `Collectors.mapping(Function<? super T, ? extends U> mapper, Collector<? super U, A, R> downstream)`.

```java
package com.bank.report;

import java.util.List;
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

public class NameRoster {
    public static Map<String, List<String>> namesByDepartment(List<Employee> employees) {
        return employees.stream()
                .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(
                        Employee::getDepartment,
                        Collectors.mapping(Employee::getName, Collectors.toList())));
    }
}
```

### Why do we need mapping instead of mapping the stream first?

If you map the stream to names before grouping, you throw away the `Employee` object and with it the department, so you can no longer group by department. `Collectors.mapping` solves this by keeping the grouping on the full `Employee` and only reducing to the name inside each group.

```java
// WRONG: once mapped to names, the department is gone, so you cannot group by it.
// This does not even compile as a one-liner, and the intent is lost.
List<String> names = employees.stream()
        .map(Employee::getName)   // Employee -> String, department discarded
        .collect(Collectors.toList());
// names is a flat list with no department information at all.
```

```java
// CORRECT: group on the full Employee, reduce to name only inside each group.
Map<String, List<String>> result = employees.stream()
        .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(
                Employee::getDepartment,
                Collectors.mapping(Employee::getName, Collectors.toList())));
```

### How does mapping work internally?

`Collectors.mapping` wraps the downstream collector's accumulator. When the stream offers an element, mapping first applies the mapper, then calls the downstream accumulator with the mapped value. The identity, combiner, and finisher all come from the downstream collector unchanged.

Conceptually the accumulator is `(container, element) -> downstream.accumulator().accept(container, mapper.apply(element))`. Nothing about the grouping changes: each department still gets its own downstream container, and mapping simply transforms each element on the way in.

```java
package com.bank.report;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class Employee {
    private final String name;
    private final String department;
    private final String gender;
    private final BigDecimal salary;

    public Employee(String name, String department, String gender, BigDecimal salary) {
        this.name = name;
        this.department = department;
        this.gender = gender;
        this.salary = salary;
    }

    public String getName() { return name; }
    public String getDepartment() { return department; }
    public String getGender() { return gender; }
    public BigDecimal getSalary() { return salary; }
}
```

### How do you run a full example?

Here is a complete, runnable program at the National Bank of India that builds the department-to-names roster.

```java
package com.bank.report;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

public class NameRosterDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Employee> employees = List.of(
                new Employee("Rajesh", "IT", "M", new BigDecimal("1200000")),
                new Employee("Amit", "IT", "M", new BigDecimal("900000")),
                new Employee("Priya", "Finance", "F", new BigDecimal("1100000")),
                new Employee("Pankaj", "Finance", "M", new BigDecimal("1500000")));

        Map<String, List<String>> namesByDept = employees.stream()
                .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(
                        Employee::getDepartment,
                        Collectors.mapping(Employee::getName, Collectors.toList())));

        namesByDept.forEach((dept, names) ->
                System.out.println(dept + " -> " + names));
    }
}
```

```bash
javac com/bank/report/Employee.java com/bank/report/NameRosterDemo.java
java com.bank.report.NameRosterDemo
```

```text
Finance -> [Priya, Pankaj]
IT -> [Rajesh, Amit]
```

### How is mapping different from Stream.map?

Both transform elements, but `Stream.map` runs over the whole stream before anything is grouped, while `Collectors.mapping` runs inside each group as part of a collector.

| Aspect | Stream.map | Collectors.mapping |
|--------|-----------|--------------------|
| Where it runs | On the stream pipeline, before collect | Inside a downstream collector, per group |
| Keeps grouping key | No, it replaces the element entirely | Yes, grouping happens on the original element |
| Typical use | Transform then collect a flat result | Transform the stored value within a group |
| Composes with groupingBy | Only before grouping (loses the key) | As the downstream of groupingBy |

```java
// Stream.map: flat transform, no grouping possible afterwards.
List<String> allNames = employees.stream()
        .map(Employee::getName)
        .collect(Collectors.toList());

// Collectors.mapping: transform inside each department group.
Map<String, List<String>> namesByDept = employees.stream()
        .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(
                Employee::getDepartment,
                Collectors.mapping(Employee::getName, Collectors.toList())));
```

### How is mapping different from flatMapping?

`Collectors.mapping` maps one element to one value. `Collectors.flatMapping` (added in Java 9) maps one element to a stream of values and flattens them into the downstream, which is useful when each employee has multiple accounts or roles to collect.

```java
// mapping: one employee -> one name.
Map<String, List<String>> names = employees.stream()
        .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(
                Employee::getDepartment,
                Collectors.mapping(Employee::getName, Collectors.toList())));

// flatMapping: one employee -> many account numbers, flattened per department.
Map<String, List<String>> accountsByDept = employees.stream()
        .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(
                Employee::getDepartment,
                Collectors.flatMapping(e -> e.getAccountNumbers().stream(),
                        Collectors.toList())));
```

### What are common mistakes?

The most frequent mistake is mapping before grouping and then being surprised the department is gone. A second mistake is forgetting the downstream collector argument, since `mapping` always needs both a mapper and a downstream.

```java
// WRONG: mapping needs a downstream collector, this does not compile.
// Collectors.mapping(Employee::getName)

// WRONG: grouping the names instead of the employees loses the department.
Map<String, List<String>> broken = employees.stream()
        .map(Employee::getName)
        .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(name -> name)); // keyed by name, not department

// CORRECT: both arguments present, grouping on the Employee.
Map<String, List<String>> correct = employees.stream()
        .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(
                Employee::getDepartment,
                Collectors.mapping(Employee::getName, Collectors.toList())));
```

### What are the edge cases?

A classifier that returns null throws a `NullPointerException` from `groupingBy`. An empty input stream produces an empty outer map, not an error. If the mapper itself returns null, the null is stored in the list, which may surprise downstream readers.

```java
// Edge case: null department crashes grouping.
List<Employee> withNullDept = List.of(
        new Employee("Rajesh", null, "M", new BigDecimal("1200000")));
// employees.stream().collect(groupingBy(Employee::getDepartment, ...))
// throws NullPointerException because the key is null.

// Safe fix: default the key before grouping.
Map<String, List<String>> safe = withNullDept.stream()
        .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(
                e -> e.getDepartment() == null ? "UNKNOWN" : e.getDepartment(),
                Collectors.mapping(Employee::getName, Collectors.toList())));
```

### How do you get sorted, de-duplicated names per department?

Because the inner collector is free, swap `toList()` for `toCollection(TreeSet::new)` to get names sorted alphabetically and with duplicates removed per department.

```java
import java.util.Set;
import java.util.TreeSet;

Map<String, Set<String>> sortedUniqueNames = employees.stream()
        .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(
                Employee::getDepartment,
                Collectors.mapping(Employee::getName,
                        Collectors.toCollection(TreeSet::new))));
```

```text
Finance -> [Pankaj, Priya]
IT -> [Amit, Rajesh]
```

### When should you use mapping, and when not?

Use `Collectors.mapping` whenever you must group by one property but store a different, derived value per group. Do not use it when you actually need the whole element in each group, since plain `groupingBy` already gives `Map<String, List<Employee>>`. Do not use it to transform the entire stream when grouping is irrelevant, because `Stream.map` is clearer there.

```java
// Use mapping: group by department, store only names.
Map<String, List<String>> names = employees.stream()
        .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(Employee::getDepartment,
                Collectors.mapping(Employee::getName, Collectors.toList())));

// Do NOT use mapping: you want the whole Employee per group.
Map<String, List<Employee>> fullGroups = employees.stream()
        .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(Employee::getDepartment));
```

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

Interviewers often ask how `mapping` differs from `map`, why mapping before grouping loses the key, what happens on a null classifier, and how to change the per-group collection type. A strong answer mentions that `mapping` composes with any downstream, including nested groupingBy, counting, or joining, and that the finisher of the downstream still runs, so `mapping(Employee::getName, Collectors.joining(", "))` would give a comma-joined name string per department.

```java
// Follow-up: a joined name string per department.
Map<String, String> joinedNames = employees.stream()
        .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(
                Employee::getDepartment,
                Collectors.mapping(Employee::getName,
                        Collectors.joining(", "))));
```

```text
Finance -> Priya, Pankaj
IT -> Rajesh, Amit
```
